# <%= it.scope %>/testing

For micro-frontends: test helpers that wrap a component in the providers of the shell. Everything in
[`@mfeflow/testing`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/testing), plus the company's list of providers and one
render helper that uses it. It is installed with [`<%= it.scope %>/runtime`](../runtime), where the providers are
composed. `@testing-library/react`, `react` and `react-dom` are peer dependencies. Leave this package out of a repo
whose apps do not run a shell.

## What to fill in

`src/defaults.ts` holds `defaultProviders`, the same list the shell passes to `composeProviders`. It is empty, so
until you fill it in a component is rendered without providers.

`renderWithCompany(ui, options?)` is `renderWithPlatform` with that list. A `providers` in `options` replaces it.
`resetPlatform()` clears the page-wide state between tests.

```tsx
import { renderWithCompany, resetPlatform } from '<%= it.scope %>/testing';

afterEach(resetPlatform);

it('shows the title', () => {
  const { getByText } = renderWithCompany(<Title />);
  expect(getByText('Orders')).toBeTruthy();
});
```

## Settings only, no state

`<%= it.scope %>/testing` is not a Module Federation singleton, so every remote gets its own copy of this package.
That is fine while it holds only a list of providers and a function that renders with it. Keep it that way: no
rendered tree, mock or flag at module level here.
